      body: '{{img:0}}<h3>What Albuquerque homeowners actually pay</h3><p>Albuquerque prices by the hour, from HomeBlue\u2019s Albuquerque cost page (materials extra):</p><table class="price-table"><thead><tr><th>Handyman time in Albuquerque</th><th>Typical price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 hour</td><td>$40–$80</td></tr><tr><td>2 hours</td><td>$80–$150</td></tr><tr><td>4 hours (half day)</td><td>$160–$300</td></tr><tr><td>8 hours (full day)</td><td>$320–$610</td></tr></tbody></table><p>New Mexico figures line up with that range. Housecall Pro puts solo handymen in New Mexico at <strong>$50–$90 an hour</strong>, with a minimum service charge of <strong>$75–$150</strong> common across the state. So the low Albuquerque hourly number is real, but a one-hour job rarely bills at one hour. The minimum is the floor.</p><p>For one-off jobs, national flat prices give you a rough check before you call (HomeGuide, national): TV mounting <strong>$100–$300</strong>, furniture assembly <strong>$85–$200</strong>, a ceiling fan install <strong>$75–$150</strong>, drywall repair <strong>$200–$800</strong>, and stucco repair <strong>$200–$800</strong>. Those are national numbers, not Albuquerque quotes — use them to spot a quote that is way off, not as the price you should pay here.</p>{{img:1}}<h3>What changes the price in Albuquerque?</h3><p><strong>The list, or the lack of one.</strong> One sticking door in 87112 is a minimum-charge visit. That same door plus a drywall patch, a loose gate latch, and two curtain rods is one trip and one setup. Albuquerque homes collect the same small jobs — stucco cracks, a cooler pad change in spring, a door that swells and sticks after the monsoon rains. Write them down as they happen and hand over the whole list at once.</p><p><strong>Adobe, stucco, and flat roofs.</strong> A lot of Albuquerque houses are stucco over block or frame, and patching stucco so it blends is slower than patching plain drywall. Flat-roof homes also turn a small ceiling stain into a look-up-top-first job. None of that is exotic. It just means a Northeast Heights stucco patch can take longer than the national drywall number suggests.</p><p><strong>Licensed work is not handyman work in New Mexico.</strong> This one matters here. New Mexico\u2019s handyman exemption excludes electrical, plumbing, and gas fitting — that work needs a licensed trade, whatever the size of the job. A handyman can hang the ceiling fan you bought. Rewiring the box it hangs from is a licensed electrician\u2019s job. If a quote folds real electrical or plumbing work into a cheap hourly rate, that is a reason to pass, not a bargain.</p><p><strong>Distance across town.</strong> Albuquerque is spread out. A handyman based in the Northeast who drives to the Westside or down to the South Valley burns unpaid time in the truck, and some charge for it. The two ZIPs our featured listing covers — 87112 in the northeast and 87113 on the far north side — are close together, which is part of why local pros price by the area they actually work.</p><h3>How to pay less without a worse job</h3><ul class="check-list"><li><strong>Bundle the list.</strong> One four-hour visit at $160–$300 costs less than four separate one-hour calls, each hitting a minimum.</li><li><strong>Buy the parts yourself when you can.</strong> Nationally, a handyman who buys materials may add a 20% to 50% markup, plus a trip charge (HomeGuide, national). The faucet, fan, or latch sitting by the door when he arrives removes both.</li><li><strong>Ask the price shape before he starts.</strong> Hourly, flat per job, or hourly plus a minimum — all three are normal. Not knowing which one you agreed to is where most bad bills come from.</li></ul><h3>An Albuquerque handyman listed on Van Squads</h3><p><strong>The ABQ Handyman</strong> is a handyman business listed on Van Squads, offering handyman work at your location in Albuquerque — ZIPs 87112 and 87113, in northeast and far north Albuquerque. See its listing and request a quote: <a href="/business/the-abq-handyman">The ABQ Handyman on Van Squads</a>.</p><p>Van Squads lists 7 handyman businesses in the Albuquerque metro, covering ZIPs 87112 and 87113. Enter your ZIP to see who covers your neighborhood: <a href="/#/browse?zip=87112">search handyman near 87112</a>.</p><p>Want the whole list on one page? <a href=\"/handyman/albuquerque/\">Handyman in Albuquerque</a> has every company Van Squads lists there, with phone numbers and service areas.</p><p>Need the house cleaned too? See the Albuquerque house cleaning cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-house-cleaning-cost-in-albuquerque/">how much does house cleaning cost in Albuquerque?</a></p>{{img:2}}<div class="disclosure"><strong>Sources.</strong> HomeBlue — Handyman Prices in Albuquerque, New Mexico (average Albuquerque hourly rate $40–$80; 1 hour $40–$80, 2 hours $80–$150, 4 hours $160–$300, 8 hours $320–$610, plus materials; emergency work, licensed-trade work, permits, and hard-to-reach locations raise the price) (homeblue.com); Housecall Pro — Handyman License in New Mexico: 2026 Requirements & Cost (independent handymen in New Mexico commonly bill $50–$90/hr; NM pros commonly set a $75–$150 minimum service charge; New Mexico\u2019s handyman exemption excludes electrical, plumbing, and gas fitting work, which requires a CID trade license) (housecallpro.com); HomeGuide — 2026 Handyman Hourly Rates (national flat prices: TV mounting $100–$300, furniture assembly $85–$200, ceiling fan installation $75–$150, drywall repair $200–$800, stucco repair $200–$800; minimum charge $75–$200, trip charge $30–$80, material markup 20%–50%+ — national figures, labeled national) (homeguide.com).</div>'
